<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sankin-kōtai</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as sankin-kotai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sankin-kōtai, or alternate attendance, was a system of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a> requiring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> to divide their time between their domains and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a>'s capital at Edo, leaving their families in Edo as hostages. The costly journeys and households drained the lords' resources and limited their ability to rebel. The system also stimulated roads, towns and commerce across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sankin-kotai.jpg" alt="Sankin-kōtai" width="320" height="157" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: a former retainer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tokugawa_Seiseiroku_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
